WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
What the measurements say about current agents.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:06.467
Three lines of published evidence are worth knowing, each with its limits.

3
00:00:06.747 --> 00:00:07.941
Time horizons.

4
00:00:08.221 --> 00:00:18.973
METR (Kwa and colleagues, 2025) proposed measuring agents by the length of tasks, in human expert time, that they complete with 50% success.

5
00:00:19.253 --> 00:00:26.016
They timed skilled people on software and research tasks, then fit each model’s success rate against task length.

6
00:00:26.296 --> 00:00:28.088
At publication, Claude 3.

7
00:00:28.368 --> 00:00:40.485
7 Sonnet’s 50% horizon was around 50 minutes, and frontier horizons had been doubling roughly every seven months since 2019, possibly faster in 2024.

8
00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:51.411
METR’s post adds that models of the time succeeded nearly always on tasks under about four minutes and under 10% of the time on tasks over about four hours.

9
00:00:51.691 --> 00:00:58.112
METR has published updated measurements since; check their site for current figures rather than relying on these.

10
00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:12.109
Toby Ord (May 2025) noticed that METR’s curves are roughly what a constant hazard rate would give: a fixed chance of failure per minute of task, so success decays like a half-life, S(t) = 0.

11
00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:14.587
5 to the power t over T50.

12
00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:24.552
That is the compounding model in continuous time, and it predicts the horizon at success level q is T50 times ln q over ln 0.

13
00:01:24.832 --> 00:01:30.933
5: about a third of T50 at 80%, and about 1/70 at 99%.

14
00:01:31.213 --> 00:01:42.008
Ord’s own 2026 update notes later analysis suggesting hazard rates decline over the course of a task, so treat the half-life as a first approximation.

15
00:01:42.288 --> 00:01:51.504
Either way, the lesson for production is the same: the task length an agent handles at the reliability you need is much shorter than its headline horizon.

16
00:01:51.784 --> 00:01:53.576
Reliability across trials.

17
00:01:53.856 --> 00:02:06.443
tau-bench (Yao and colleagues, 2024), which you met in Studio 3, simulates customer-service conversations and measures pass^k, the chance that all k attempts at a task succeed.

18
00:02:06.723 --> 00:02:16.621
At publication, even strong function-calling agents succeeded on fewer than half the tasks, and pass^8 in the retail domain was below 25%.

19
00:02:16.901 --> 00:02:23.472
Every user is a new trial, so pass^k is closer to what customers experience than a single-run score.

20
00:02:23.752 --> 00:02:24.733
Real work.

21
00:02:25.013 --> 00:02:40.501
METR’s randomized trial (Becker and colleagues, 2025) had 16 experienced open-source developers complete 246 real tasks in their own repositories, with AI tools allowed or disallowed at random.

22
00:02:40.781 --> 00:02:49.827
With early-2025 tools allowed, tasks took 19% longer, while the developers believed AI had sped them up by about 20%.

23
00:02:50.107 --> 00:03:01.392
The authors caution against generalizing: a small sample of experts in large, familiar codebases, one period of tools, and METR has since published an update on later tools.

24
00:03:01.672 --> 00:03:06.749
The finding worth keeping is methodological: perceived speedups are not measured ones.
